<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="45976" href="schedule/2014/spring/JOUR/451/45976.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2014" href="schedule/2014.xml">2014</calendarYear><term id="120141" href="schedule/2014/spring.xml">Spring 2014</term><subject id="JOUR" href="schedule/2014/spring/JOUR.xml">Journalism</subject><course id="451" href="schedule/2014/spring/JOUR/451.xml">Research Methods in Journalism</course></parents><sectionNumber>A  </sectionNumber><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>The course introduces students to the theoretical principles on which scientific research is based and the techniques for doing scientific research in journalism and mass communications. It provides the critical framework for evaluating numerical information reported in the media.   Note:  The JOUR 200 pre-requisite has been waived, however, students must complete a Quantitative Reasoning I course to be eligible to register for this course.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2014-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2014-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>3</roomNumber><buildingName>Gregory Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Sotirovic" firstName="M">Sotirovic, M</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>